A||||4||rmcmsw||TH-125C conversion to 4T60 transmission||||1||
Z||000000||rmcmsw||08-12-2001||12:31 PM||||All,<BR>This message is really a question for Karl Hamilton but maybe some of you might already know the answers.<BR>1. The article listed at the web address <A HREF="http://spacecoastfieros.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://spacecoastfieros.com/</A>  entitled, "Fiero TH440/4TH60 Automatic Transmission Conversion" is worth noting. It is something I have felt possible to accomplish for over a decade but have been unable to find anyone either technical enought or brave enough to try it until now. The benefits are hugh!<BR>2. Karl, if your out there, I would like to ask you some questions or anyone else for that matter?<BR>a. Is your conversion still working ok?<BR>b. Have you had any problems in the area where you said. "The 4T60 has several gear activated switches but it is not possible to use them because only one side of the switch is wired to the connector. The other side of the switches is internally grounded to the transmission case." Or, have you come up with a soultion for this?<BR>c. Did you use the gear selector from a Grand Prix or other 4 speed automatic car? The gear shift plate on the Fiero could be changed by removing the plastic between P and R and putting in a new indicator. A 1989 Grand Prix comes to mind.<BR>d. What axel shafts did you use and if you had to modify them, what was the process? <BR>There is a place, here in New Orleans, that makes and sells racing torque converters and automatic transmission and I'll take the article to them for comment and suggestions. I know you can pick up a 89 or 90 4T60 pretty cheap and I would have these guys rebuild and modify it. I don't think I would want to get any earlier version of the 4T60 for there were a lot of problems with that gear box in it's early years of production. I have a list of all of the service bulletins regarding it. I hope I can get some answers to these questions before I begin my quest. I don't want to get a gear box, rebuild and modify it, install it, and find it doesn't always work. It's not so much the expense but the waste of time and effort.<BR>Thanks in advance for all the input.<BR>Regards,<BR>Mike Nichols<BR>Command Master Chief, USN, Retired<BR>86.5 GT 3.1 Modified (232hp)<BR>1958 Austin Healey 100-6MM 2 seater <BR>1995 Callaway Corvette Ultra-Motion 450 6-speed roadster (452hp)<BR>1999 GMC Denali modified & supercharged (473hp) "The Beast"<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||pontiacs4me||08-12-2001||01:44 PM||||Welcome to the forum Mike,  I was wondering kind of the same thing.  I am going to be doing a 3.4 swap this winter, and Ill be replacing my th125 with a 4t65 or 4t65e.  I was wondering if the mounting locations are the same, or what it takes to mount this transmission into my subframe.  What about axleshafts, are the the same, or will I have to have customs made?  What about wiring?  Im going with a haltech computer system, so the stock computer will be chunked.  Thanks for any additional info.||||reg||1
